it's great to be joined today by luke  vargas who is united nations bureau  chief for talk media news he has joined  us over the years from all over the  world and today we are talking about  Donald Trump and climate change the  one-year anniversary of the Paris  climate agreement is today which has  been a source source of much discussion  from Donald Trump do we even have a  sense now Luke of exactly where Trump  stands on climate change of course the  ranging from leading a climate-science  denier to had the EPA and suggesting  that climate change is a hoax created by  the Chinese to saying that hey he  believes in science and he'll listen to  people Ivanka Trump even meeting with Al  Gore where does Trump stand on this  right now we honestly don't know where  he stands he's been all over the place  for instance in 2009 he signed this open  letter to President Obama which ran in  the New York Times other people it's  called the business leaders for  environmental change letters Ben and  Jerry signed it the CEO of seventh  generation that fancy paper company that  sells at Whole Foods this is not the  crowd that Donald Trump rolls with now  and both he his sons and ivanka I'll  sign this letter which among other  things called for President Obama in  2009 to go to those Copenhagen climate  talks that Hillary Clinton talked about  so much in the primary and to deliver  robust emissions targets binding ones  that was a bit beyond what the Paris  agreement now does this is a big call  from him the letter went on to say if we  fail to act now it is a scientifically  irrefutable that there will be  catastrophic and irreversible  consequences for humanity and our planet  this is so far away from what Donald  Trump is saying now and much in the way  that he was sort of running to the left  of the American political establishment  on issues in the past like health care  at a time when Democrats would never  talk about single-payer it seemed like  Donald Trump just six years ago was  running to the left of the American  mainstream on climate change as well so  suffice it to say this guy is change  things around he is not only appointing  people to the cabinet who are  detrimental to the cause of climate  change action but saying things which  are also very unproductive and on top of  that threatening to remove the United  States from the Paris agreement so  across the board  Donald Trump has gone through a  tremendous reversal of positions um and  even just a few hours ago telling Fox  News that he really questions the  science behind climate change this is a  360 turn for Donald Trump 180 turn I'm  sure you mean 360 would get him right  back in the sale a source or yet uh so  we could he could end up there of course  protractors aside for a second though uh  I want to get into in a second the  specifics of what Trump could do with  regard to the Paris agreements but  before we do that generally every time  we hear about a trump pick for cabinet  or advisor or whatever one of the main  questions is okay here's what they  believe but how much could they  unilaterally do when it comes to actual  federal government actions what's the  worst case for actual climate policy the  worst case scenario looks to be  something close to what we have right  now which is that you have dangerous  appointees and very unhelpful public  statements on the appointee front we're  talking about putting someone to chair  the EPA who wants to diminish the power  of governmental regulations on a mission  someone in the body of Oklahoma Attorney  General Scott Pruitt someone at the  State Department who's not going to use  that office to go around the world like  Hillary Clinton or John Kerry didn't  promote multilateral action on climate  change but someone like CEO Rex  Tillerman a possible Secretary of State  nominee who will try to resurrect the  power of the fossil fuel industry and  squeeze whatever profit still be found  out of that industry and then finally  giving power this is maybe not big names  would be doing this but it's something  that could be accomplished across the  scope of an administration people at the  EPA people at the gioi in the White  House at NASA at the National Institutes  of Science in other places as credit  climate science that's been produced by  past administrations will discredit the  scientists the people who are on the  government payroll at NASA in other  places studying receiving public funds  to study the effects of climate change  and either as some people worry actually  forcing them out of a job or sort of  more passive action just denying them  research funding denying them government  platforms in which to spread that  research on the statement side the  people surrounding Donald Trump also  know that unhelpful public statements  when it comes to the Paris agreement  when it comes to just the scientific  consensus on climate  change actively can help prop up a  misinformation campaign we heard Donald  Trump just you know on Fox News say  nobody really knows if climate change is  real that's precisely the kind of talk  that emboldened climate change deniers  and lends credibility to their websites  which as of now don't have much  institutional credit credibility and and  finally threatening to remove the u.s.  from the Paris agreement is a great way  to discredit that agreement as a whole  and to prevent a deal from taking off at  a time when it was gathering a lot of  momentum for the election so let's talk  now in more detail about that Paris  agreement Trump aside he's gonna get us  out of it the agreement has already  become international law it goes fully  into effect in 2020 what could Trump  unilaterally do I mean there is a  withdrawal process but we're not just  talking about a deal that the u.s. is  the only party to I mean this is complex  stuff right so one option that's been  talked about a lot is that you just  remove the u.s. from the Paris agreement  much like article 50 of the European  Union treaty which takes two years for  brexit to actually happen there is a  four year window at delay so Donald  Trump on day one were to remove the u.s.  from Paris agreement to basically wait  out his entire term before that became  finalized the faster alternative is to  take the 1992 Senate ratified treaty for  the UNF Triple C the sort of framework  of UN climate negotiations and just  pulled the US the u.s. out of that that  only has a one year windows so that  could be done you know after his first  year in office finally though he could  formally do nothing to those treaties  but in practice do a lot to basically  cause the u.s. to fall far short of the  pledges that made as a part of the Paris  agreement basically open new coal mines  I start emitting more a prop up offshore  drilling eliminate emissions regulations  these in the US could even keep showing  up at UN climate talks he could kind of  troll the talks by you know sending an  oil CEO or something to go there and you  know get a lot of flack from the rest of  the world but just be defiant the  climate scientists I talked to a worried  the most about that because at the end  of the day it is actions on climate  change and emissions that matter more  than words and basically blocking the  implementation of these  spirit of the law of the Paris agreement  would do the most damage in the limited  time we have left Luke China now really  getting involved in a serious manner in  climate negotiations and but potentially  as one of the main drivers of curbing  emissions and mitigating climate change  caused by humans what can we gather from  that what what does what do things look  like with China potentially taking the  lead on climate I think it's highly  likely that they will take the lead on  climate china's energy and its  environmental policies always take the  lead from chinese national security  interest that's all you've seen them  opening up coal plants and shutting them  down sometimes making aggressive claims  in the South China Sea in order to  guarantee that oil can keep being  imported to China through the Straits of  Malacca but Chinese national security  interests can also be helpful for the  planet it can cause them to close down  coal mines it can cause them to address  air pollution because the Chinese  population is increasingly concerned  about the health problems from that and  finally as the sort of de facto leader  of the g77 those industrialized  countries particularly in South and  Latin America and Africa there's a  potential for China to see the business  value in trying to sell the developing  world on the solar panels and things  that it makes and I'm not surprised to  hear people trying to tell Donald Trump  this is a 21st century technology green  technology that you can win for the  United States don't fight for 19th  century and 20 20th century coal and oil  we don't want to lose the 21st century  green energy revolution to China and  that's why Leonardo DiCaprio and others  are going to his office trying to sell  him on the business argument for climate  change and and they may be successful  that's the way this could work the one  good piece of news is that no matter  what Trump says about coal right now the  low price of natural gas makes makes  coal not a particularly attractive way  to go but that is that that is a  temporary thing and that can absolutely  change we've been speaking to luke  vargas united nations bureau chief for  talk media news Luke always great to  talk to you thank you

